WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:07.729
Let's go next to our caller from the 6 0 3
area code who's calling today from 6 0 3.

00:00:07.729 --> 00:00:10.350
Hi, my name's Eric, New Hampshire.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.110
Hey, Eric, what's up?

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:13.110
Not much.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:21.119
I just have a quick question because of the
pandemic.

00:00:21.119 --> 00:00:27.840
How wider will the separation between classes
become without any policy or tax change?

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:33.559
If, say, Trump gets reelected and.

00:00:33.559 --> 00:00:39.210
You know, everything becomes the way it has
been for the past three years.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:42.700
How far do you think the haves and have nots.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.670
That separation will grow?

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:48.030
I don't have a mathematical answer, but it's
going to grow.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:49.030
And you're out.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:50.070
No, no asking.

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:55.059
You're asking a really important question,
which is we are now seeing, you know, that

00:00:55.059 --> 00:00:59.870
there's this implicit understanding of blue
collar and white collar that exists.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:07.750
You know, the idea sometimes is made very
simple by calling it office work versus factory

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:08.750
work.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:11.770
The reality is it's much more complex than
that right now.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:17.170
You see, as I mentioned, for example, airline
pilots very much affected by what's going

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:18.250
on right now.

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:24.690
You see people who work in restaurants, but
also people who work in certain types of retail.

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:29.500
And even people in certain areas of health
care that are being hit because they are typically

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:33.110
non-emergency health care, et cetera.

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:38.340
So we are going to say I was growing.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:39.340
Yeah.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:47.110
I was more talking about the people that the
shareholders of the airlines, not the pilots.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:48.110
Yeah.

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:53.221
I mean, I'm talking about the ones that have
the wealth.

00:01:53.221 --> 00:02:00.820
You are going to seize opportunities, whether
you know, but not not the person that goes

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:04.740
to work, the ones that tell the people to
go to work.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:05.740
Absolutely.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:06.740
Absolutely.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:07.740
Listen in.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:12.830
At any time there is a cataclysm, a crisis,
a pandemic, a natural disaster, something

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:14.450
like 9/11.

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:20.720
These are opportunities for speculators and
those who have are flush with cash to make

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:26.200
a ton of money with speculative and nonproductive
use of assets.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:27.750
There is no doubt about it.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:29.970
And you're completely accurate in that.

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:34.920
There is going to be a widening income gap,
a wealth gap, rather, as a result of this,

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.960
because of people who are in a position to
seize some of those opportunities.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:38.960
Totally.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:39.960
OK.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:40.960
Right.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:47.360
I mean, that's why I thought unless there
is policy or tax change, of course, too.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:48.360
So like one example.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:54.710
Now this is starting to Trump actually floated
this, but he didn't do it, which is what about

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:59.310
suspending the payroll tax for a certain period
of time?

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:04.530
The Social Security, Medicare, that would
actually be an interesting thing, because

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:07.750
if you don't remember exactly where the line
is, but it's around one hundred and twenty

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:13.480
five thousand a year, you only pay Social
Security, Medicare up to your first hundred

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:16.610
and twenty five thousand a year in wages.

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:21.440
So people who earn a lot more than that in
wages or people who are earning their money

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:24.390
through capital gains, they're already not
paying that.

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:29.700
So suspending that would be a way to directly
do fiscal stimulus to people who are actually

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:31.170
mostly working for a living.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:32.530
It wasn't done right.

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:35.200
But that's an example of a policy that will.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.069
Yeah, I agree.

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:38.319
All right.

00:03:38.319 --> 00:03:40.780
So much for your call.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:42.560
I very much appreciate it.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:47.470
And it's great to hear from our mostly friendly
neighbors to the north in New Hampshire.

